        <strong>earliest-dated</strong> appearance of this text that I’ve
        found.
      </mark>
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="same-text">
    <h2>This is the same text as the Circassian Center profile</h2>
    <p>
      The body of this entry is, word for word as far as I can see, the
      <a href={longVersion}>Circassian Center profile “Emin Semsuğ”</a>, down
      to the same list of works and the same spelling slip (“Semguğ” for his
      father). The Circassian Center page and
      <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/biyografi-net-emin-semsug/">Biyografi.Net</a>
      carry it with later dates, and the
      <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/yeni-akit-emin-semsug/">Yeni Akit entry</a>
      (27 October 2016) looks like a shortened version of it. So the others
      are probably copies of this one, or of whatever it came from, and
      <strong>none of them is an independent source</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because it is someone else’s published work, I haven’t translated it in
      full. The Circassian Center page has the full summary, the translated
      passages and the review items, and I haven’t repeated them here. Please
      have a Turkish reader check my translations.
    </p>
    <h3>What differs</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The page gives only the name as the title, with no summary line and no
        “writer, publisher” label (which Biyografi.Net added).
      </li>
      <li>
        The text is one block, with the works list run into the same
        paragraph. It uses “öldü” (“died”) for his death, as Circassian Center
        does.
      </li>
      <li>
        A comment section at the bottom says “nobody has commented yet”.
      </li>
      <li>
        The page image is a generic placeholder, not a photo of him.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="review">
    <h2>Things to review</h2>
    <ul class="review">
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>unknown origin.</strong> Even the oldest copy has no
          author and no source. The text reads like an encyclopedia entry
          (“Eserleri”, “Works”), and may come from a printed reference book, but
          I haven’t found it. Whoever it came from is the real source of every
          claim below, and no one on these pages can be checked.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>claims that conflict with other sources on this
          site.</strong> The same ones as on the Circassian Center page:
          life dates (1900–1952 against 1900–1953 or 1903–1952), the birthplace
          (Mansure against Quneitra), the studies in France against Mirzoev’s
          Beirut and the Sorbonne, the 1937 trading company against “Jolan”,
          the newspaper’s dates and languages, the place and year of his books
          (Homs 1948 against Amman 1984), the school details, and the
          unsourced family history and Druze mediation.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>spelling.</strong> The title says “Semsuğ”, but the
          text calls his father “Semguğ”.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the 1980s book.</strong> The text says the
          <em>Introduction</em> was published after his death “in the 1980s”.
          That is consistent with Mirzoev’s 1984, but the text gives no place.
        </mark>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <hr />

